preConditionHook:
// THE TREND IS STEEPENING: the market's RATE of travel over the last nearBars
// bars is at least accelMultiple times its rate over the farBars bars before
// them. Grimes gives "trend rate" no formula; it reduces to a ratio of two
// displacement rates, and the book cites the steepening as a buying-climax
// sign as well as a regime read.
//
// CROSS-MULTIPLIED, so no division rounds and a zero-length far leg cannot
// raise a runtime divide-by-zero -- the MinRewardToRiskFilter spelling. The
// per-bar normalization is what lets the two windows be DIFFERENT lengths: a
// short near window measured against a longer baseline.
//
// SIDES IDENTICAL because both rates are ABSOLUTE: an absolute value carries
// no direction, so this is placed beside a directional rule rather than
// standing in for one.
//
// KEPT SEPARATE FROM AcceleratingLegsClimax, the other half of this slice's
// acceleration cluster, and the reason is not the arithmetic: that rule is
// SIGNED and MIRRORED (it requires the prior leg to run in the trade's own
// direction, which is what makes its comparison mean "steeper in the same
// direction") and it PINS the two windows equal. This one is ABSOLUTE,
// direction-agnostic, and normalizes by window length so the windows may
// differ. No setting of either rule's inputs reproduces the other's fired-bar
// set -- a down leg following a larger up leg fires this and not that -- and a
// boolean that switched BOTH the sign requirement AND the meaning of the short
// side is a larger switch than merge decision 12's one-line-ratio case covers.
// Also not ParabolicAccelerationExit, which is an Exit, signed, and
// equal-windowed.
//
// Differences of closes only, so the rule passes the shift test; no indicator
// and no persistent state.
//
// Every scratch value is a localVariables entry, never a hook-scope
// declaration, so the hooks survive a double placement.
//
// Deepest bar touched: nearBars + farBars.
nearMove = std::fabs(close[0] - close[nearBars]);
farMove = std::fabs(close[nearBars] - close[nearBars + farBars]);
